## Enhanced Solar Cells Stability by Hygroscopic Polymer Passivation of Metal
## Halide Perovskite Thin Film
\(^{1}\) Center for Nano Science and Technology @Polimi, Istituto Italiano di Tecnologia, via Giovanni Pascoli 70/3, 20133, Milan, Italy.
\(^{2}\) Dipartimento di Fisica, Politecnico di Milano, Piazza L. da Vinci, 32, 20133 Milano, Italy.
E-mail: Annamaria.Petrozza@iit.it
Abstract
Long-term device stability is one of the most critical issues that impede perovskite solar cell commercialization. Here we show that a thin layer of a functional hygroscopic polymer, poly(ethylene oxide) PEO, on top of the perovskite thin film, can make perovskite-based solar cells highly stable during operation and in a humid atmosphere. We prove that PEO chemically interacts with lead ions on the perovskite surface, thus passivates undercoordinated defect sites. Importantly, the defect healing by PEO not only results in an improvement of the photo-voltage but also makes the perovskite thin film stable. Finally, we demonstrate that the hygroscopic PEO thin film can prevent water inclusion into perovskite film by screening water molecules, thus embodying a multi-functional role. Overall, such interface engineering leads to highly durable perovskite solar cell, which, in the presence of PEO passivation, retained more than \(95\%\) of initial power conversion efficiency over 15-h illumination, under load, in the ambient atmosphere without encapsulation. Our findings experimentally reveal the role of interface engineering in mastering the instability of perovskite materials and propose a general approach for improving the reliability of perovskite-based optoelectronic devices.
## 1. Introduction
Perovskite solar cells have emerged as one of the most promising photovoltaics by virtue of its highly improved power conversion efficiency (PCE) up to \(22\%\) within a few years.\(^{1-3}\) The solution-processable perovskite photovoltaic module presents the shortest energy payback period of all solar technologies, less than a year.\(^{4-6}\) However, for further developments, there still exist challenges related to their high susceptibility to moisture and operational instability under continuous illumination.\(^{7}\)
Degradation of perovskite materials can be caused by humidity, oxygen, light, and heat.\(^{7,8}\) In most of the studies, water has been considered as the primary cause of the instability of the perovskite because most of the perovskite elements, alkylammonium salts and some lead salts, are hygroscopic.\(^{9}\) The absorbed water in perovskite layer acts as a catalyst in the whole degradation process and thereby speeds up the decomposition of perovskite. Especially \(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}\) \(\left(\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}\right)\) readily reacts with water to form intermediate byproducts, monohydrate, and insulating dihydrate under continuous water exposure, which eventually decomposes into \(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{I}\) and \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\).\(^{10,11}\) \(\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}\) perovskite films readily undergo degradation when exposed to light and oxygen together.\(^{12}\) The abundant ionic vacancy defects in the perovskite structure can be catalytic sites for photo-induced superoxide formation and further degradation.\(^{12,13}\)
The water-induced degradation processes of perovskite films can be inhibited by proper device encapsulation with hydrophobic barrier layers. However, effective water barrier layers often require multiple complicated processes or expensive organic-inorganic materials to completely prevent water vapor permeation into the perovskite devices.\(^{14-16}\) The use of polymeric hydrophobic barriers, just on top of the semiconductor layer, such as polystyrene\(^{17,18}\) and carbon materials\(^{19}\), has been suggested to relax the constraints of the encapsulating materials. To protect perovskite from water penetration, the hydrophobic barrier layer should form complete film coverage without pinholes. However, depositing a hydrophobic layer on a hydrophilic perovskite could lead to the formation of thick films possibly with pinholes due to unfavorable surface interactions.\(^{20,21}\) These thick and insulating barriers on perovskite film could also induce high series resistance in the photovoltaic device, so they should be incorporated with additional charge-conductive networks.\(^{22,23}\) In this regard polymer intermixing in the perovskite bulk phase has been proposed to obtain high stability of perovskite device by improving the morphology of
the perovskite films. Various polymers, e.g., poly(ethylene glycol)\(^{24}\), poly(methyl methacrylate)\(^{25}\), poly(4-vinylpyridine)\(^{26}\), and semiconducting polymers\(^{27}\), were introduced into the perovskite film to achieve humidity resistance by encapsulating grain boundaries of perovskite crystallites in the film. However, processing polymer additive into the perovskite layer needs sophisticated optimizations because the blend system could go through harsh phase separation due to its thermodynamic driving force. Moreover, it is not a universal approach for a variety of perovskite precursors and device structures.Interface engineering of perovskite surfaces by inserting an interfacial layer between the perovskite and charge transport layers has been attracting interests to improve device stability at large.\(^{28}\) The interfacial layers can prevent undesirable chemical reactions, and they keep the capacity of carrier transport at the interface.\(^{29, 30}\) These modifications of the perovskite interfaces serve not only as encapsulants against the environment but play multi-functional roles, acting as a semiconductor or charge tunneling layer, reducing carrier recombination at the interface and protecting the perovskite from degradation.\(^{31}\) Recently, W. Tress et al. have shown that light-soaking impairs solar cell performance by generating surface defects becoming a new source of non-radiative recombination.\(^{32}\) They conclude that improving long-term perovskite stability needs some focus on adjacent layers as well. Therefore, we need to study more about which properties of interfacial layers could be effective in improving the stability of perovskite.Here, we design the embodiment of a polymer thin film as an interlayer between the perovskite and the charge extracting layers to improve the environmental- and operational stability of perovskite solar cells. To investigate how the hygroscopic polymer influences moisture-related instability of perovskite, we chose two representative polymers: a hygroscopic polymer, polyethylene oxide (PEO), which absorbs water before the perovskite layer and a hydrophobic polymer, polystyrene (PS), which repels water molecules from the perovskite layer. Although both PEO and PS have been already embodied in perovskite solar cells, there has been no comparative study to understand what makes an effective protection of the perovskite layer.\(^{17, 24}\) We characterized the polymer thin film formation on perovskite, the intermolecular interactions between the polymer and the perovskite film, and their role in the protection of perovskite thin films. We show that the deposition of PEO at the perovskite interface reduces the density of trap states in the semiconductor thin film, prolongs the carrier lifetime, and enhances the \( V_{oc} \) of the
planar-structured perovskite devices, therefore increasing the PCE. This strategy eventually can enhance the operational stability of photovoltaic devices, resulting in extended lifespan under continuous illumination in ambient conditions.
## 2. Results and discussion
## 2.1. Polymer film formation
We chose two polymers, which have different water-compatibilities, hygroscopic PEO and hydrophobic PS (Fig. 1a), and measured water contact angle (WCA) of the polymer films, which are \(\sim11^{\circ}\) for PEO and \(\sim90^{\circ}\) for PS (Fig. S1). Prepared from polymer solutions in chlorobenzene, the polymer thin films were spin-coated on the \(\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}\) perovskite layer. The concentrations of the polymer solutions were controlled in the range from 0.1 to \(10\;\mathrm{mg\cdot ml^{-1}}\). With the polymer concentration less than \(10\;\mathrm{mg\cdot ml^{-1}}\), the processed polymer films on perovskite would be a few to tens of nanometers thick, which is unlikely to form a complete coverage on perovskite film. Therefore, to estimate the degree of polymer coverage on perovskite, \(\varphi\), based on their chemical surface composition, we measured X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS). A very thin polymer layer on perovskite with incomplete coverage exhibits a Pb signal in its XPS spectra. To calculate the degree of polymer coverage \(\varphi\), the relative atomic concentration (at \%) of Pb (the key element of the perovskite layer) are compared before and after polymer deposition by using the equation, \(\varphi=1-[P b]_{1}/[P b]_{0}\), where \([P b]_{1}\) is at% of Pb on polymer-coated perovskite film and \([P b]_{0}\) is at% of Pb on pristine perovskite film (Fig. S2, Table 1, and Table S1). The measured area irradiated by X-ray was \(\sim2\;\mathrm{mm}^{2}\). However, these values could be underestimated because the film thickness of a few nanometers is thin enough for emission of photoexcited electrons from the underlying perovskite layer. With processes from \(1\;\mathrm{mg\cdot ml^{-1}}\) polymer solutions, we measure that \(\varphi\) were 0.32 and 0.35 for PEO and PS on perovskite, respectively. Processed from \(10\;\mathrm{mg\cdot ml^{-1}}\) polymer concentrations, \(\varphi\) is close to one, which indicates complete polymer coverages on perovskite films. We obtained SEM and AFM images of polymer-coated \(\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}\) perovskite films to investigate the polymer film formations on perovskite (Fig. 1b and Fig. S3). The top-view SEM image for the neat perovskite film shows closely packed grains of perovskite crystals with the size between
200 and \(500\ \mathrm{nm}\). The perovskite films which are covered with PEO or PS thin films show smoothed surface morphologies and become obscure due to flattened topologies by polymer infiltrations. The cross-section SEM images show that the \(\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}\) perovskite crystals are conformally covered with polymer thin film, PEO or PS, and the thicknesses of polymer thin films are about 30 to \(40\ \mathrm{nm}\) when they are processed from \(10\ \mathrm{mg\cdot ml^{-1}}\). The AFM images of PEO-coated and PS-coated perovskites also show smoothened topologies with lower average surface roughnesses: \(\mathrm{R_{a}}=31.9\ \mathrm{nm}\) for the bare \(\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}\), \(16.4\ \mathrm{nm}\) for PEO-\(\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}\), and \(12.7\ \mathrm{nm}\) for PS-\(\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}\) (Fig. S3).
## 2.2. Degradation kinetics under high humidity
We obtained optical images of perovskite films degrading at high relative humidity (RH) of \(88\%\) over two weeks (Fig. 2a). We chose solutions with a high polymer concentration of \(10\ \mathrm{mg\cdot ml^{-1}}\) in chlorobenzene to form a complete coverage on perovskite. We also scratched the edge of the perovskite films before polymer coating to completely cover every side of the perovskite with the polymer film. As the pristine perovskite is exposed to the humid atmosphere, the black color of the perovskite phase changes to the transparent color, indicating a transition to the hydrated phase. In the optical images of the pristine perovskite film without polymer encapsulation, it is observed that small transparent spots appear at the 2nd day, and then grow continuously. The number of the hydrated transparent spots continuously increase during hydration all over the surface. The perovskite film with PS encapsulation also shows the emergence of transparent spots at the 3rd days, which keep growing into a large size. Compared to the pristine film without polymer encapsulation, the perovskite film with PS seems to have a lower number of initial nucleation sites which triggers hydration and degradation, but at the 15th day of exposure, they both degrade significantly. We calculate a ratio, \(\xi\), of the white area of the hydrated phase to the black-colored area of the perovskite phase in the optical images by using a graphic tool (Fig. S4), which can be related to the degree of hydration. After 15-day aging, \(\xi\) calculated from the bare \(\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}\) film is \(0.68\), and \(\xi\) from the PS-\(\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}\) is \(0.70\). On the other hand, the PEO-\(\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}\) shows a completely different behavior with respect to the reference and PS-coated perovskite sample. The PEO-\(\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}\) film does not change its aspect even after 15 days, showing very low \(\xi\) of \(\sim0.02\). At the 3rd day, we notice a small white hydrated spot that possibly
nucleated from a pinhole on the PEO film, but this spot does not grow on a large scale over 15 days. The UV-Vis spectra of each film before and after degradation also support these observations (Fig. 2b and 2c). The degree of decrease of the absorption coefficient is proportional to the area of hydrated transparent area. The spectra of the PEO-perovskite show little difference over 15 days of water exposure (it reaches a saturation point after 10 days, then it starts a slow degradation, Fig. S5) while the pristine and the PS-perovskite exhibit a continuous degradation, which agrees with the changes in the appearance of the films. More importantly, even with lower polymer coverage, obtained by depositing the thin film from lower polymer concentrations solutions \( (1 - 0.1 \, \mathrm{mg \cdot ml^{-1}}) \), similar degradation behaviors are observed (Fig. S6 and Fig. S7). We thus confirm that the PEO coated perovskite films show enhanced durability regardless of its surface coverage on perovskite, while the PS-passivated perovskite films do not exhibit a protective functionality when exposed to RH as high as \( 88\% \).
In order to get more detailed information, we also collected structural data on perovskite phases during the hydration process by X-ray Diffraction (XRD) measurements on the exposed to RH of \( 88\% \). The XRD patterns of the three perovskites with and without the PEO and PS polymer passivation before and after moisture exposure were measured. The diffraction pattern of the as-prepared perovskite films exhibits the tetragonal perovskite phase, crystallizing in the I4cm space group (Fig. 2d). As hydration proceeded, two additional peaks appear as a consequence of hydration, which is related to the formation of the hydrated perovskite complex and \( \mathrm{PbI}_{2} \) at \( 10.5^{\circ} \) and \( 12.7^{\circ} \), respectively.\(^{10, 34}\) Focusing on these two diffraction peaks, different behaviors are observed depending on the polymer coverage. For the as-prepared perovskite layers, the diffraction peak at \( 10.5^{\circ} \) which corresponds to the hydrated perovskite phase is observed after moisture exposure for one day (Fig. 2e). Later, after a 4-day exposure to high RH, the hydrated perovskite phase disappears, and instead, higher signal intensities for the peak of \( \mathrm{PbI}_{2} \) phase are found. This result means that the as-prepared perovskite degrades through hydration and then decomposes into \( \mathrm{PbI}_{2} \) and MAI phases. Similar behavior is also found for \( \mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}/\mathrm{PS} \) (Fig. 2g). In this case, the intensity of the peaks attributed to the hydrated perovskite and \( \mathrm{PbI}_{2} \) is slightly reduced by \( 60\% \) when compared to the bare perovskite after moisture exposure, but the transition from the hydration to decomposition is seen in a similar manner. On the other hand, the perovskite passivated with PEO does not show any change of perovskite phases into hydrated or
decomposed phases, which indicates that MAPbI\(_3\)/PEO is highly durable even under high RH of \(88\%\) over at least 4 days (Fig. 2f).
## 2.3. Defect passivation by polymer thin film
Interfaces formation can lead to defect formation and the appearance of new electronic states. Therefore, it is crucial to monitor the optoelectronic quality of the interface upon the development of the device architecture. The photoluminescence dynamics are strongly sensitive to defect density which affects radiative and nonradiative carrier decay paths. Figure 3a shows the photoluminescence spectra of three identical MAPbI\(_3\) films, two of them interfaced with PEO or PS thin films. To exclude the effect of oxygen in the air, the samples were kept and measured in vacuum (pressure \(<10^{-5}\) mbar). The samples were monochromatically excited at \(560\mathrm{nm}\), about the center of the solar spectrum, with a power density of about \(100\mathrm{mW}/\mathrm{cm}^{2}\), close to the solar excitation conditions. This allows to properly evaluate the emission properties of the thin films, taking into account the intensity dependence of the emission efficiency due to trap filling phenomena (see Fig S9 in the SI). The PEO-capped perovskite is about twice more emissive compared to the reference sample, while the PS-capped perovskite film is similar or even less emissive than of the reference sample. Consistently, the time-resolved PL dynamics, probed at the emission spectra peaks, show that the carrier lifetime of PEO-capped perovskite becomes much longer with respect to that of the bare perovskite thin film (Fig. 3b, Table S2). These results infer that the PEO thin polymer heals defect states on the thin film surface by effective suppression of non-radiative recombination paths. On the other hand, the PL lifetimes are shorter in the PS-capped film, suggesting the formation of furthers non-radiative recombination center at the interface between the two materials.
Fourier-transform Infrared Spectroscopy (FTIR) is used to verify the presence of intermolecular interactions between perovskite and polymer thin layer. First of all, we have excluded the role of the polymer terminal groups in the passivation process, as by changing the polymer molecular weight we do not observe significant difference on the interface properties (Fig. S10). In Figure 4.a the IR spectra show that the C-O stretching mode at \(1060~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) of bare PEO film shifts to a lower frequency, \(1030~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\), when it is interfaced to the PEO-MAPbI\(_3\) film. This peak shift is ascribed to the formation of a coordinate bond between PEO and the perovskite film by sharing
the lone electron pair on the C-O of PEO with the empty 6p orbital of \(\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}\) in perovskite. (Fig.S11)
To obtain an in-depth understanding of the chemical interaction between perovskite and polymer thin film, XPS measurements were carried out (Fig. 4b and 4c). Core level scans of Pb4f for each sample are compared. The Pb4f core level of the pristine sample contains two features, one at higher binding energy (142.8 eV) and the other at lower binding energy (138 eV). However, the PEO-coated sample exhibits additional peaks at lower binding energy (141 and 136 eV), which correspond to Pb-O. It is attributed to the coordinate bonding between Pb(II) from perovskite and O from the PEO. On the other hand, the PS-coated sample shows no difference from the pristine perovskite, which means that PS does not have a chemical interaction with perovskite. These XPS results suggest that PEO polymer interacts with perovskite forming a Pb-O bond which would passivate the anion vacancies. It was reported that deep trap sites of perovskite could be passivated by oxygen from the atmosphere. However it cannot stabilize the material on long-term. In this study, we demonstrate that PEO passivates defects which can directly or indirectly lead to non-radiative recombination in the \(\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}\), with an approach which is technologically relevant.
## 2.4. Device characteristics and operational stability
Figure 5 shows the photovoltaic device characteristics with and without the polymer passivation on perovskite film. We fabricated photovoltaic devices whose structure was a standard architecture, FTO/compact \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{C}_{60}/\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}/\mathrm{PEO}\) or PS passivation/spiro-OMeTAD/Au. By varying polymer concentrations, we could control the degree of polymer coverage, \(\varphi\), from 0.21 to 0.99 to optimize the device performances. The photovoltaic parameters of \(\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}\) devices are summarized in Table 2 and Figure 5a. The \(\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}/\mathrm{PEO}\) device with \(\varphi\) of 0.21 shows an enhancement of PCE on average compared to the reference \(\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}\) device (see also Fig. 5d). However, the \(\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}/\mathrm{PEO}\) device with high \(\varphi\) of 0.97 exhibits a significant reduction of PCE due to a low fill factor and big hysteresis, which is attributed to high series resistance and the reduced tunneling current through insulating PEO interlayer. It should be noted that the \(V_{oc}\) of \(\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}/\mathrm{PEO}\) device increases from 1.05 to 1.10 V with a PEO coverage, \(\varphi = 0.21\) (Fig. 5b). This increase in \(V_{oc}\) well reflects the defect-passivation by PEO which was anticipated by PL
studies. The EQE spectra also match a slight increase in \( J_{sc} \) of PEO device from 19.6 to 19.9 mA·cm\(^{-2}\) (Fig. 5c). On the other hand, the MAPbI\(_3\)/PS devices do not show an increase in \( V_{oc} \) contrary to the PEO devices (see Fig. 5d for statistical behavior of devices performances). This is in agreement with the spectroscopic studies that show no interaction of PS with perovskite and the formation of interface defect states. The PS based devices show comparable photovoltaic performances when using formulation leading both to high and low coverage on pristine thin films (Fig. S12). These results indicate that the PS polymer thin film forms efficient charge-tunneling layer even with a full polymer coverage in contrast to PEO devices. To elucidate the charge tunneling properties, we measured dark \( J_{-}V \) curves of the MAPbI\(_3\) devices with PEO or PS thin films (Fig. S13). The MAPbI\(_3\)/PEO devices show a clear reduction of on-current at the applied voltage of \( 1.4~\mathrm{V} \) as increasing surface coverage of PEO, while the MAPbI\(_3\)/PS devices show a negligible reduction of on-current. This efficient charge tunneling property of PS film has also been reported.\(^{17, 42}\) We speculate that the hydrophobicity of PS polymer might induce incomplete conformal coverage on the perovskite with some nanoscale pinholes.\(^{21}\) To visualize the PS film formation on perovskite, we measured AFM image of PS-MAPbI\(_3\), which shows that PS polymer does not form a conformal film on the perovskite crystals, still having uncovered area exposed to the exterior (Fig. S14). This PS film formation would result in not only efficient charge tunneling through \(\sim30\)-nm-thick insulating PS film,\(^{46}\) but also water penetration through it, finally driving a degradation of the MAPbI\(_3\)/PS device in a humid environment. On the other hand, the AFM image of PEO film displays that PEO polymer completely covers the surface of perovskite crystals, in a conform way, which could be attributed to intermolecular interaction between PEO and perovskite.
To examine the effect of the PEO-passivation on device stability, we performed aging tests of high-performance non-encapsulated perovskite devices in controlled conditions. To investigate hydrational degradation, we exposed the devices to the high humid environment (RH = 88%) in the dark and measured periodically, at each instance exposed to light for 1 min at the MPP (Fig. 6a). The reference device shows an accelerated, continuous degradation of PCE from 15% to 1% in 120 h. The slope of degradation became steeper after the 3rd day due to the accelerated decomposition of PbI\(_2\) and MAI. This behavior is identically observed for the PS-capped device with PS coverages, \( \varphi = 0.35 \) and \( \varphi = 0.99 \). However, PEO-capped devices with PEO coverages of only \( \varphi = 0.32 \), shows only a 20% loss over 120-h exposure to humid air (RH = 88%).
Importantly, these degradation trends mirror those reported for the thin films absorbance at \(700\mathrm{nm}\) (Fig. 6.a and 2 and Fig. S5), inferring that the slower degradation rate is the result of the slowing down of the perovskite hydration process. To further understand the role of PEO in device protection from hydration, we also tested this film passivated by poly(methyl methacrylate) (PMMA), another hydrophilic polymer, which also show a polar moiety, but not so hygroscopic as PEO. It show similar degradation behavior as the reference sample (Fig S8 of SI). Then, we fabricated a complete device where the PEO layer is on top of the Au electrode, instead of between the electrode and the perovskite layer. We examined its shelf-life stability upon exposure to \(88\%\) RH over seven days, which shows that the over-coated PEO layer successfully protects perovskite device from hydration-driven degradation. (Fig. S15). This result suggests that the hygroscopic property of PEO polymer has a relevant contribution to the stability of the device.
## 3. Conclusions
We have proposed an effective strategy of interface engineering for improving the stability of perovskite solar cells. We use a hygroscopic polymer, PEO, as an interlayer between the perovskite and charge extracting layer which allows for reproducible and high-quality deposition on the hydrophilic perovskite. The hygroscopic polymer strongly slows down the perovskite hydration process by absorbing water before perovskite does. Spectroscopic studies demonstrate that the lone electron pairs of PEO polymer bond with undercoordinated Pb ions on the perovskite surface perovskite. This results in the passivation of defect sites and the reduction of the loss channels. These two beneficial aspects of PEO, protecting perovskite from hydration and passivating defects, improve operational device stability under exposure to high humidity, illumination and polarization. In fact, we demonstrated highly durable perovskite solar cell with PEO passivation which retained more than \(95\%\) of initial power conversion efficiency over 15-h illumination, under load, in the ambient atmosphere without encapsulations. This study provides not only a fundamental understanding of degradation mechanism of perovskite but also a promising strategy to protect the perovskite layer by using a functional polymer layer.
## 4. Experimental section
Materials: All materials were purchased and used as received. \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{PbBr}_{2}\) were purchased from Alfa Aesar. Methylammonium iodide (MAI) and formamidinium iodide (FAI) were obtained from Dyesol. Cesium bromide, N, N-dimethylformamide (DMF), dimethylsulfoxide (DMSO), polystyrene (PS, \(\mathrm{M}_{\mathrm{W}}\sim180{,}000\)) and chlorobenzene (anhydrous) were purchased from Sigma Aldrich. Spiro-OMeTAD was from Luminescence Technology Corp. Polyethylene oxide (PEO, \(\mathrm{M}_{\mathrm{W}}\sim10{,}000\)) was obtained from Dae Myung Chemical, Korea. Film fabrication: FTO-coated glass was etched with zinc powder (Zinc powder, Sigma Aldrich) and 2M aqueous HCl solution for electrode pattern. The FTO substrates were sequentially washed with \(2\%\) Hellamanex in water, deionized water, iso-propanol, acetone, iso-propanol in a sonication bath for \(\sim15\,\mathrm{min}\), followed by \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) plasma cleaning for \(10\,\mathrm{min}\). A patterned and cleaned transparent conducting oxide substrate was covered with a \(\sim30\,\mathrm{nm}\)-thick \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) compact layer by spin-coating of a titanium diisopropoxide (Sigma Aldrich) solution in 2-propanol (0.05
M) at \(450\ \ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). The \(\mathrm{C}_{60}\) layer was deposited from a \(\mathrm{C}_{60}\) solution \((10\ \mathrm{mg\cdot ml^{-1}})\) in 1,2-dichlorobenzene by spin-coating at \(3000\ \mathrm{rpm}\), followed by thermal annealing at \(100\ \ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(10\ \mathrm{min}\).For the \(\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}\) film, a \(1.4\ \mathrm{M}\) solution of \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{MAI}\) (1:1 molar ratio) in dimethylformamide (DMF) and dimethyl sulfoxide (DMSO) at a volume ratio of 9:1 was deposited on the \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{FTO}\) substrate by spin-coating, and then \(0.1\ \mathrm{mL}\) of toluene was dropped on the rotating substrate. The resulting transparent film was annealed on a hot plate at \(100\ \ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(10\ \mathrm{min}\).The mixture cation \(\mathrm{FA}/\mathrm{Cs}\) perovskite was prepared by dissolving different molar quantities of \(1.21\ \mathrm{M}\ \mathrm{PbI}_{2}\), \(1.21\ \mathrm{M}\ \mathrm{FAI}\), \(0.24\ \mathrm{M}\ \mathrm{PbBr}_{2}\), and \(0.24\ \mathrm{M}\ \mathrm{CsBr}\) in \(1\ \mathrm{L}\) solvent of \(\mathrm{N}\), \(\mathrm{N}-\mathrm{Dimethylformamide}\) (DMF) and dimethyl sulfoxide (DMSO) at a volume ratio of 4:1.For polymer passivation, we prepared PEO and PS solution in chlorobenzene with various concentrations from \(0.1\) to \(10\ \mathrm{mg\cdot ml^{-1}}\). The polymer solutions were deposited on the perovskite film by spin-coating at \(4000\ \mathrm{rpm}\). And then the polymer/perovskite film was dried for \(10\ \mathrm{min}\) in the glove box.For photovoltaic device fabrication, a spiro-MeOTAD solution was spin coated on the perovskite layer at \(4000\ \mathrm{rpm}\) for \(30\ \mathrm{s}\). Spiro-MeOTAD solution was prepared by dissolving \(73\ \mathrm{mg}\) of spiro-MeOTAD in \(1\ \mathrm{mL}\) chlorobenzene \((99.8\%\); Sigma–Aldrich), to which were added \(28.8\ \upmu\mathrm{L}\) of 4-tert-butylpyridine \((96\%\), Sigma-Aldrich), \(17.5\ \upmu\mathrm{L}\) lithium bis(trifluoromethanesulfonyl)imide (LiTFSI) solution \((520\ \mathrm{mg}\ \mathrm{LiTSFI}\) in \(1\ \mathrm{mL}\) acetonitrile, \(99.8\%\), Sigma-Aldrich). This fabrication process was carried out under controlled conditions in a glove-box atmosphere. Finally, \(75\ \mathrm{nm}\) gold was thermally evaporated on top of the device at a pressure of \(1\ \times\ 10^{-6}\) mbar to form the back contact. The active area of the complete device was \(0.08\ \mathrm{cm}^{-2}\).CharacterizationsUV–Vis absorption spectra are recorded with an UV–Vis Varian Cary 5000. Perovskite films were prepared on bare glass substratesXRD patterns were recorded with a Bruker D8 Advance diffractometer with Bragg-Brentano geometry equipped with a \(\mathrm{Cu}\ \mathrm{K}\alpha_{1}\) \((\lambda=1.544060\ \mathrm{\AA})\) anode, operating at \(40\ \mathrm{kV}\) and \(40\ \mathrm{mA}\). All
the diffraction patterns were collected at room temperature over an angular range \(2\theta = 8^{\circ} - 60^{\circ}\), with a step size of \(0.02^{\circ}\) in a symmetric \(2\,\theta\,/\omega\) scan reflection mode and acquisition time of \(1\;\mathrm{s}\).The FT-IR spectra of the perovskite films deposited on silicon substrates are measured using a PerkinElmer 2000 FTIR spectrometer, by exploiting a transmission mode. Measurements are carried out in the \(100\,-\,4000\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) range. The substrate contribution is removed from the measured data.X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy spectra were taken by X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (ESCALAB 250Xi, Thermo Scientific) using monochromated Al Ka source (1486.6 eV).SEM images were obtained using a JCM-6010LV, JEOL at \(15{-}20\;\mathrm{kV}\) electron beam.The contact angle measurements were performed using OCA 15 EC system. The values were determined by dropping a volume of \(1~\upmu\mathrm{L}\) of water on the different substrates using the sessile drop method. Digital images were captured with a CCD-camera (resolution of \(752\times852\) pixels), and the values of contact angle were extracted using the SCA-20 software.Steady-state Photoluminescence: Excitation light was provided by a CW diode laser (Oxxius laserboxx, wavelength \(560\;\mathrm{nm}\)). \(\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}\) films were illuminated at different intensities between \(10^{-2}\) and \(1~\upmu\mathrm{J}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\), which correspond to photon densities between \(\sim10^{20}\) to \(10^{22}\;\mathrm{cm}^{-3}\). Samples were mounted inside a vacuum chamber (pressure \({<}10^{-5}\) mbar), and PL was collected in reflection mode and focused into a fiber coupled to a spectrometer (Ocean Optics Maya Pro 2000).Time-resolved Photoluminescence: Time-resolved PL measurements were performed in a Time Correlated Single Photon Counting (TCSPC) setup. The excitation source was an unamplified tunable Ti:Sapphire laser (Coherent Chameleon Ultra II, temporal and spectral bandwidths of \(\sim140\) fs and \(\sim5\;\mathrm{nm}\), respectively), which was tuned at a central wavelength of \(700\;\mathrm{nm}\). The measurements were performed with excitation at \(2\;\mathrm{MHz}\), which was achieved using an acousto-optical modulator pulse picker (APE Pulse Select). The samples were mounted in a chamber under vacuum, the PL was collected in reflection mode and focused onto a spectrometer coupled to a \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) cooled photomultiplier.Photovoltaic characterization: The current density-voltage (J-V) characteristics were measured with a computer-controlled Keithley 2420 source meter in air without any device
encapsulation. The simulated Air Mass 1.5 Global (AM 1.5G) irradiance was provided with a class AAA Newport solar simulator. The light intensity was calibrated with a silicon reference cell with a spectral mismatch factor of 0.99. For the J-V measurement, the scan rates were 0.05 V/s for slow scans and \(0.2\;\mathrm{V/s}\) for fast scans. The forward scan started from \(0\;\mathrm{V}\) (the short circuit condition) to \(1.4\;\mathrm{V}\), while reverse scan from \(1.4\;\mathrm{V}\) to \(0\;\mathrm{V}\). The pre-conditional stress was not done for PV measurements. The stabilized maximum power output was recorded for more than 90 s. The EQE was measured with a home-built setup. EQE spectra were recorded using the monochromated (Bentham) output from a tungsten halogen lamp calibrated with a Newport UV-818 photodiode. For humidity degradation test, we controlled relative humidity in a closed glass jar where water was vaporized from a saturated salt solution of potassium chloride. We kept perovskite films and photovoltaic devices in the glass jar under relative humidity of \(88\pm1\%\) in the dark at room temperature. We measured RH value of the chamber in which we kept perovskite films and devices by using a hygrometer.
